{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The St Ilven Hospital lost its protected status because it was used to commit acts harmful to the enemy that are not excluded by the exemptions in Additional Protocol I.",
      "reasoning": "Article 13(1) of Additional Protocol I provides that protection ceases when a civilian medical unit is used to commit acts harmful to the enemy, and the loss of protection is not prevented by the limited exemptions listed in paragraph 2 (light individual weapons, pickets, small arms taken from the wounded, or the presence of combatants for medical reasons). The Drennick forces used the hospital’s top floor as an artillery observation post, mounted a heavy machine gun on the roof, and stored artillery ammunition in the basement—activities that constitute direct support of hostilities and are not among the exempted activities. Accordingly, the hospital’s protection under both the Fourth Geneva Convention and Additional Protocol I ceased.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "api_i",
          "article": "13",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian medical units are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian function, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after a warning has been given setting, whenever appropriate, a reasonable time-limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "api_i",
          "article": "13",
          "quoted_text": "The following shall not be considered as acts harmful to the enemy: (a) that the personnel of the unit are equipped with light individual weapons for their own defence or for that of the wounded and sick in their charge; (b) that the unit is guarded by a picket or by sentries or by an escort; (c) that small arms and ammunition taken from the wounded and sick, and not yet handed to the proper service, are found in the units; (d) that members of the armed forces or other combatants are in the unit for medical reasons."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The required warning was duly given on 9 August 2025, with a reasonable 48‑hour time‑limit that remained unheeded, satisfying the condition for loss of protection under the Geneva Conventions and Additional Protocol I.",
      "reasoning": "Both Article 19 of the Fourth Geneva Convention and Article 13 of Additional Protocol I stipulate that protection may cease only after a due warning setting a reasonable time‑limit has been issued and remains unheeded. On 9 August Calvaran forces broadcast a radio warning, distributed leaflets, and delivered a notice through an ICRC delegate, demanding cessation of the hospital’s military use within 48 hours. The Drennick forces continued to operate the observation post, machine gun, and ammunition storage on 10 and 11 August, thereby failing to comply with the warning. The deadline expired on 11 August, after which the hospital’s protection could lawfully be considered lost.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "19",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian hospitals are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian duties, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after due warning has been given, naming, in all appropriate cases, a reasonable time limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "api_i",
          "article": "13",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian medical units are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian function, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after a warning has been given setting, whenever appropriate, a reasonable time-limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Once its protected status ceased, the hospital became a legitimate military objective, and the 13 August air strike complied with the principles of distinction and proportionality because it targeted only the portions of the building used for hostile acts and caused no civilian casualties.",
      "reasoning": "Article 18 of the Fourth Geneva Convention establishes the general rule that civilian hospitals may not be attacked, but this rule is qualified by Article 19, which allows loss of protection when the conditions identified in Article 13 are met. Having satisfied those conditions, the hospital no longer enjoyed immunity from attack. The strike was directed at the roof and top floor—where the machine gun and observation post were located—and employed guided munitions to avoid the lower floors where no civilians remained. Because no civilians or protected medical personnel were present, the attack satisfied the distinction requirement, and the absence of civilian harm demonstrates compliance with proportionality.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "18",
          "quoted_text": "Civilian hospitals ... may in no circumstances be the object of attack, but shall at all times be respected and protected by the Parties to the conflict."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "19",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian hospitals are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian duties, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after due warning has been given, naming, in all appropriate cases, a reasonable time limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Even assuming any residual protection for medical personnel, the evacuation of all patients and staff prior to the strike eliminated any risk to protected persons, rendering the attack proportionate and therefore lawful.",
      "reasoning": "Article 20 of the Fourth Geneva Convention obliges parties to respect and protect persons engaged in the operation and administration of civilian hospitals. However, the protection attaches to the individuals, not to the building itself once the building has lost its protected status. The facts show that by 12 August all patients and medical staff had left through the safe corridor, and the hospital director confirmed that no protected persons remained. Consequently, the attack could not have caused civilian harm, satisfying the proportionality test and confirming the legality of the strike.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "20",
          "quoted_text": "Persons regularly and solely engaged in the operation and administration of civilian hospitals, including the personnel engaged in the search for, removal and transporting of and caring for wounded and sick civilians, the infirm and maternity cases, shall be respected and protected."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

